Great, so the ouput actually looked like it did a few things:
- generating PKI and tokens
  - NOTE: I love to see this, so much work already done here, and if I'm right this is the TLS end to end encryption that is often a pain to setup with vinilla kubernets cli like `kubeadm`.
- creating state directory in "~/.talos/clusters/devTalosCluster"
  - NOTE: I made note of this directory "~/.talos" and as I suspected - some juicy details for the cluster are in this directory. There are two dirs, both created by the talosctl:
    - `~/.talos/clusters/devTalosCluster/state.yaml`: tracks containerIds, bridge network (10.5.0.0/24) and port mappings. Also tracks control plane node(s) and worker node(s). This helps `talosctl` know what to do with future commands for this specific cluster. When we move to RasPi5, this will likley be very different. 
    - `~/.talos/config`: this is similar to how ~/.kube/config tells kubectl how to talk to the cluster. This tells talosctl how to talk with Talos Linux. This also does the port forwarding for the port 50000 (apid) - see below. Something else I noted, was the ED25519 PRIVATE KEY, which is not a SSH key, rather an mTLS cert for my local MAC, which is just better security for issuing OS commands to the cluster.
  - NOTE: I also found [this](https://docs.siderolabs.com/talos/v1.14/platform-specific-installations/local-platforms/docker#create-the-cluster) `talosctl config info`
- downloading ghcr.io/siderolabs/talos:v1.14.5
  - NOTE: pulling the image from github container registry (ghcr)
- creating network devTalosCluster
  - NOTE: I believe based on this [doc](https://docs.siderolabs.com/kubernetes-guides/cni/flannel), its the Flannel CNI for the network.
- creating controlplane nodes
